ing  so i had a huge but to me it was just  exciting in the stream in in the extreme  well we we get all of us to get around  at um on these  we just take it for granted we're flying  at 30 35 000 40 000 feet at tremendous  speeds  last year alone  at one airport  o'hare in chicago  70 million people flew in and out of  that one airport  70 million  and we're sitting there and does anybody  and i was  flying on  on a flight to the west coast  and i was working on a chapter of my  book in the plane and i thought  how about this  and i could have known or will write  in other words it didn't happen all that  long ago as history goes  he died in 1948 i would have been 15  years old he might have been that nice  old fellow around the corner who i love  to talk to  you uh  you ended your book this way i want to  ask you when you decided to do this on  july 20th 18 i mean excuse me 1969 when  neil armstrong another american born and  raised in southwestern ohio stepped onto  the moon he carried with him in tribute  to the wright brothers a small swatch of  the muslim  uh muslin from a wing of their 1903  flyer yep at what point did you decide  that was the way you're going to end  your book soon as i read about it  there's just something so marvelous  about that the homage he's paying  his sense of  history his sense of indebtedness to the  courage of those predecessors those  pioneers these were pioneers  pioneers of the age we live in  and i don't it's to me it's fascinating  that neil armstrong  also came from that same corner of ohio  southwestern ohio  a matter of only 50 miles or so from  where the wright brothers grew up and  that john glenn also came from ohio now  whether that's coincidental or something  in the water out there in ohio i don't  know but i love that idea  and the fact that a pittsburgher is  paying tribute to ohio is significant  yes but never forget that the aluminum  that was the genius of their motor that  they built for the first airplane came